[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should notifications be delivered to the user? → Both, in-page canonical (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last big question before I lay out approaches. You asked for notifications, but there are no tasks yet — nothing to
  have a due date, nothing to change. I don't want to quietly design a whole task manager under the banner of
  "notifications," so this is your call.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how much task substrate this effort builds.

  - [1mMinimal substrate, same spec[0m — add just enough task model to make notifications real: title, due date, done flag,
    localStorage persistence, and a bare add/list/complete UI. Explicitly thin — no editing, filtering, priorities,
    tags, or sorting. Buys you something you can actually run and test end to end. Costs a modest amount of
    non-notification work inside this spec, and the thin UI will want replacing later.
  - [1mTwo specs, tasks first[0m — decompose: spec one is the task manager, spec two is notifications on top. Cleanest
    separation and the task layer gets designed on its own merits instead of as a notification fixture. Costs an extra
    full design cycle before any notification exists.
  - [1mNotifications against an interface, test fixtures only[0m — define the task shape the notifier consumes, build the
    subsystem against it, and use throwaway fixtures to test. Keeps this spec purely about notifications. But the app
    still shows an empty page at the end; nothing is demonstrable until a task layer arrives.

  [1mMy recommendation: minimal substrate in the same spec.[0m The substrate is genuinely small at this scope, and the
  alternative buys a full extra design cycle for something the notification work needs anyway. I'd write the substrate
  into the spec as an explicitly-thin dependency with a named interface, so a richer task layer later replaces the UI
  without touching the notifier.
